{
 "id": "2026-10-25-float-point-03",
 "topic": "why 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.30000000000000004 in floating point",
 "subject": "computing",
 "hook_type": "wrong-answer reveal",
 "caption": "0.1 has no finite binary expansion, so a 64-bit double stores a value slightly above it, and the same for 0.2. Their exact sum lands exactly halfway between two neighbouring doubles, and round-half-to-even picks the upper one: 0.30000000000000004.\n\nShare this with a programmer friend.\n\n#programming #computerscience #python #javascript #floatingpoint #coding #math #stem #softwareengineering",
 "cover_at": 1.0,
 "hook": "Ask a computer for 0.1 + 0.2. It won't say 0.3.",
 "beats": [
  {
   "text": "Type it into Python or JavaScript, and this is what comes back.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "q",
     "tex": "0.1 + 0.2",
     "size": 90,
     "at": [
      0,
      4.4
     ]
    },
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "q2",
     "tex": [
      "=",
      "0.3000000000000000",
      "4"
     ],
     "size": 70,
     "next_to": "q",
     "buff": 0.4,
     "colors": {
      "2": "red"
     }
    },
    {
     "sfx": "thud"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "The adding isn't the problem. Computers count in binary, and in binary, one tenth never ends.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "b1",
     "tex": [
      "0.1",
      "=",
      "0.000110011001100\\ldots_2"
     ],
     "size": 60,
     "at": [
      0,
      1.6
     ],
     "colors": {
      "0": "blue",
      "2": "blue"
     }
    },
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "b2",
     "tex": [
      "\\tfrac13",
      "=",
      "0.333333\\ldots_{10}"
     ],
     "size": 60,
     "next_to": "b1",
     "buff": 0.5,
     "colors": {
      "0": "teal",
      "2": "teal"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Just like one third in decimal. So a 64-bit double has to round it, and stores a value slightly too big.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "s1",
     "tex": [
      "\\text{stored } 0.1",
      "=",
      "0.1 + 5.55\\times10^{-18}"
     ],
     "size": 56,
     "next_to": "b2",
     "buff": 0.7,
     "colors": {
      "2": "yellow"
     }
    },
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "s2",
     "tex": [
      "\\text{stored } 0.2",
      "=",
      "0.2 + 1.11\\times10^{-17}"
     ],
     "size": 56,
     "next_to": "s1",
     "buff": 0.35,
     "colors": {
      "2": "yellow"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "do": [
    {
     "clear": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Now zoom in near 0.3. Doubles there are spaced $2^{-54} \\approx 5.6\\times10^{-17}$ apart. Here are two neighbours.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "numberline",
     "name": "NL",
     "range": [
      -3.5,
      3.5,
      1
     ],
     "numbers": false,
     "length": 7.6,
     "at": [
      0,
      1.6
     ]
    },
    {
     "make": "dot",
     "name": "L",
     "on": "NL",
     "at": [
      -3,
      0
     ],
     "color": "blue"
    },
    {
     "make": "dot",
     "name": "R",
     "on": "NL",
     "at": [
      3,
      0
     ],
     "color": "blue"
    },
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "Ll",
     "tex": "0.3 - 1.11\\times10^{-17}",
     "size": 44,
     "color": "blue",
     "at": [
      -2.1,
      2.4
     ]
    },
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "Rl",
     "tex": "0.3 + 4.44\\times10^{-17}",
     "size": 44,
     "color": "blue",
     "at": [
      2.1,
      2.4
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "The true 0.3 sits closer to the left one. That's what you get when you type 0.3.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "dot",
     "name": "T",
